  note      = {Edition of the Late Bronze Age (13th c.) diagnostic-omen
               manuscripts from \d{H}attu\v{s}a (Bo\u{g}azk\"oy),
               manuscripts A--N. Ingested into data/middle/diagnostic as the
               Anatolian-periphery cell: Tafeln A (21--33), B (34--39) and
               C (40--46), the three substantial tablets, Akkadian text only
               (the Hittite/Luwian interlinear glosses are excluded). The
               smaller fragments D--L are mostly traces; M is birth-omens
               (Sakikk\^u 36) and N a school tablet, both left aside.
               Wilhelm's ``Schrift und Sprache'' (pp.\ 6--18) shows the
               tablets were written by Hittite-school / Mittani-tradition
               scribes with the peripheral (`Randgebiete') syllabary, and the
               symptom-protases are written SYLLABICALLY where the Babylonian
               recension uses logograms --- so this cell is the syllabic
               extreme of the Middle period. Parallels canonical Sakikk\^u
               Tablets 3--6, 13--17, 21--22, 25--27, 29, 36, 40
               (Schmidtchen 2021)}
}

